  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>When Do Ravens Nest?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens are very early nesters. In Britain and northern Europe, nest repairs begin in January, eggs are laid February–March, and chicks hatch after 20–21 days. Young ravens fledge in May–June. In warmer southern parts of the range, the timing is similar or slightly earlier; at higher altitudes and in the high Arctic it may be delayed by several weeks.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Nesting Timeline</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Key dates for Common Raven breeding in Britain and northern Europe:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>January:</strong> established pairs visit nest sites and begin adding material. Courtship displays intensify.</li>
      <li><strong>February–March:</strong> egg laying. Clutch size 3–7 eggs, most commonly 4–6. Eggs are pale blue-green to greenish, heavily speckled with brown and grey.</li>
      <li><strong>March–April:</strong> incubation (20–21 days); female incubates; male guards territory and brings food to the female.</li>
      <li><strong>April–May:</strong> chicks hatch. Both parents provision the nestlings.</li>
      <li><strong>May–June:</strong> chicks fledge at 35–42 days old.</li>
      <li><strong>June–July:</strong> family group stays together for 4–6 weeks post-fledging before young become independent.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Variation Across the Range</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Timing shifts with latitude and altitude. In the high Arctic (Alaska, northern Canada, northern Greenland), egg laying may be delayed until April due to late snowmelt and extreme cold. In the Canary Islands and other warm, southerly parts of the Common Raven's range, nesting begins in January or even December. At high altitude (Himalayan populations, Rocky Mountain birds), mid-March to April egg-laying is typical.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The Australian raven species have a different seasonal pattern — breeding in winter/spring of the Southern Hemisphere (July–October), which corresponds to the equivalent "late winter/spring" timing relative to their climate.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
